   "text": {
    "L1": "Paper does not grow like leaves on trees. People make it from plant fibres.",
    "L2": "These fibres can come from wood, bamboo, sugarcane waste, or cotton rags.",
    "L3": "First, the plant material is broken into tiny fibres and mixed with water to make pulp.",
    "L4": "The pulp is spread thinly on a net or cloth, then pressed to squeeze out water.",
    "L5": "After pressing, the thin sheet is dried. The fibres stick together as paper.",
    "L6": "Old paper can be soaked and mashed into pulp again to make recycled paper.",
    "L7": "Using both sides and recycling saves trees, water and energy, and reduces garbage.",
    "L8": "Now tap what comes first when paper is made."
   },
